      "math_academy_id": "e-13148",
      "problem": "If $A = \\{1, 1, 2,1, 3,3\\}$, then what is $\\left|A\\right|?$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nLet's start by listing all the elements of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n1,\\qquad 2, \\qquad 3\n$$\n\nNote that only *unique* elements are counted. In other words, duplicate elements are *not* counted separately.\n\nSince $A$ has $3$ elements, its cardinality is $|A| = 3.$",
